\pard\qc\b\fs36 TaskFlow Microservice Application\par
\fs30 Configuration Management Using ConfigMaps and Secrets\par
\b0\fs22\par
\pard\ql
\b Student:\b0 ______________________________\par
\b Date:\b0 ______________________________\par
\par
\b 1. Aim\b0\par
Implement configuration management in Kubernetes using ConfigMaps and Secrets, then deploy a containerized application that consumes both non-sensitive and sensitive configuration data.\par
\par
\b 2. Application Components\b0\par
The practical uses the existing TaskFlow application:\par
\tab MongoDB\par
\tab user-service\par
\tab task-service\par
\tab stats-service\par
\tab frontend\par
\par
\b 3. Basic Concepts\b0\par
\b ConfigMap:\b0 Stores non-sensitive configuration such as environment names, ports, database hostnames, and internal Service URLs.\par
\b Secret:\b0 Stores sensitive configuration such as database usernames and passwords. Kubernetes stores Secret data encoded and does not display values through normal describe commands.\par
\b Environment Injection:\b0 Deployments consume ConfigMap and Secret values as environment variables inside containers.\par
\b Service Discovery:\b0 Kubernetes Service names such as \f1 mongodb\f0 and \f1 user-service\f0 are used instead of localhost or changing Pod IP addresses.\par
\par
\b 4. Project Files\b0\par
The configuration manifests are stored in the \f1 k8s\f0 directory:\par
\f1 01-secret.yaml\par
02-configmap.yaml\par
03-mongodb.yaml\par
04-user-service.yaml\par
05-task-service.yaml\par
06-stats-service.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 5. Kubernetes Prerequisites\b0\par
Open PowerShell in the project root and verify Kubernetes:\par
\f1 cd "C:\\Users\\ROHAN PRAJAPATI\\Desktop\\sem7\\cloud native and devops\\lab\\microservice_app\\taskflow"\par
kubectl config current-context\par
kubectl get nodes\par
\f0\par
Expected context: \f1 docker-desktop\f0 . Expected node status: \f1 Ready\f0 .\par
\par
Select the application namespace:\par
\f1 kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=taskflow\par
\f0\par
\b 6. Create the Secret\b0\par
The Secret contains MongoDB credentials. The file \f1 k8s/01-secret.yaml\f0 contains:\par
\f1 apiVersion: v1\par
kind: Secret\par
metadata:\par
  name: taskflow-secrets\par
  namespace: taskflow\par
type: Opaque\par
stringData:\par
  MONGO_USERNAME: admin\par
  MONGO_PASSWORD: adminpass\par
\f0\par
Apply the Secret:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/01-secret.yaml\par
\f0\par
For a real production system, do not commit plaintext credentials to Git. Use an external secret manager or sealed secrets.\par
\par
\b 7. Create the ConfigMap\b0\par
The ConfigMap contains non-sensitive settings:\par
\f1 apiVersion: v1\par
kind: ConfigMap\par
metadata:\par
  name: taskflow-config\par
  namespace: taskflow\par
data:\par
  NODE_ENV: production\par
  LOG_LEVEL: info\par
  MONGO_HOST: mongodb\par
  MONGO_PORT: "27017"\par
  MONGO_DB_NAME: taskflow\par
  USER_SERVICE_URL: http://user-service:4001\par
  TASK_SERVICE_URL: http://task-service:4002\par
\f0\par
Apply the ConfigMap:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/02-configmap.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 8. Configure MongoDB\b0\par
MongoDB consumes the Secret using \f1 secretKeyRef\f0 :\par
\f1 MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME -> MONGO_USERNAME\par
MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD -> MONGO_PASSWORD\par
\f0\par
Deploy MongoDB:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/03-mongodb.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 9. Configure the User Service\b0\par
The user-service Deployment consumes the ConfigMap with:\par
\f1 envFrom:\par
  - configMapRef:\par
      name: taskflow-config\par
\f0\par
It consumes sensitive credentials with:\par
\f1 env:\par
  - name: MONGO_USERNAME\par
    valueFrom:\par
      secretKeyRef:\par
        name: taskflow-secrets\par
        key: MONGO_USERNAME\par
  - name: MONGO_PASSWORD\par
    valueFrom:\par
      secretKeyRef:\par
        name: taskflow-secrets\par
        key: MONGO_PASSWORD\par
\f0\par
Apply the Deployment:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/04-user-service.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 10. Configure the Task Service\b0\par
The task-service uses the same ConfigMap and Secret references. It also receives the internal user-service URL from the ConfigMap.\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/05-task-service.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 11. Configure the Stats Service\b0\par
The stats-service consumes non-sensitive Service URLs from the ConfigMap:\par
\f1 USER_SERVICE_URL=http://user-service:4001\par
TASK_SERVICE_URL=http://task-service:4002\par
\f0\par
Apply the Deployment:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/06-stats-service.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 12. Complete Configuration Deployment\b0\par
All configuration and application manifests can be applied together:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/01-secret.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/02-configmap.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/03-mongodb.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/04-user-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/05-task-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/06-stats-service.yaml\par
\f0\par
Restart consumers after changing configuration values:\par
\f1 kubectl rollout restart deployment/user-service deployment/task-service deployment/stats-service -n taskflow\par
kubectl rollout status deployment/user-service -n taskflow --timeout=120s\par
kubectl rollout status deployment/task-service -n taskflow --timeout=120s\par
kubectl rollout status deployment/stats-service -n taskflow --timeout=120s\par
\f0\par
\b 13. Verify ConfigMap\b0\par
Display non-sensitive configuration:\par
\f1 kubectl describe configmap taskflow-config -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Expected values include:\par
\f1 NODE_ENV: production\par
MONGO_HOST: mongodb\par
MONGO_DB_NAME: taskflow\par
USER_SERVICE_URL: http://user-service:4001\par
TASK_SERVICE_URL: http://task-service:4002\par
\f0\par
Take Screenshot 1: ConfigMap data.\par
\par
\b 14. Verify Secret Safely\b0\par
Display Secret key names and byte counts without displaying values:\par
\f1 kubectl describe secret taskflow-secrets -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Expected keys:\par
\f1 MONGO_USERNAME\par
MONGO_PASSWORD\par
\f0\par
Take Screenshot 2: Secret key names and byte counts. Never include actual password values in a screenshot or report.\par
\par
\b 15. Verify Application Consumption\b0\par
Display non-sensitive environment variables inside user-service and confirm the password exists without printing it:\par
\f1 kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/user-service -- sh -c 'echo NODE_ENV=$NODE_ENV; echo MONGO_HOST=$MONGO_HOST; echo MONGO_DB_NAME=$MONGO_DB_NAME; echo USER_SERVICE_URL=$USER_SERVICE_URL; test -n "$MONGO_PASSWORD" && echo MONGO_PASSWORD=SET'\par
\f0\par
Verify the task-service also receives the Secret:\par
\f1 kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/task-service -- sh -c 'echo MONGO_HOST=$MONGO_HOST; test -n "$MONGO_PASSWORD" && echo MONGO_PASSWORD=SET'\par
\f0\par
Expected output includes:\par
\f1 NODE_ENV=production\par
MONGO_HOST=mongodb\par
MONGO_DB_NAME=taskflow\par
USER_SERVICE_URL=http://user-service:4001\par
MONGO_PASSWORD=SET\par
\f0\par
Take Screenshot 3: Application consuming ConfigMap and Secret values.\par
\par
\b 16. Verify Service Health\b0\par
\f1 kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/user-service -- wget -qO- http://localhost:4001/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/task-service -- wget -qO- http://localhost:4002/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/stats-service -- wget -qO- http://localhost:4003/health\par
\f0\par
Expected response contains:\par
\f1 {"status":"ok"}\par
\f0\par
Take Screenshot 4: Healthy configuration-consuming services.\par
\par
\b 17. Verify Pods and Deployments\b0\par
\f1 kubectl get deployments -n taskflow\par
kubectl get pods -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Expected result:\par
\f1 user-service: 3/3 Ready\par
task-service: 3/3 Ready\par
stats-service: 3/3 Ready\par
mongodb: 1/1 Ready\par
\f0\par
Take Screenshot 5: Running Deployments and Pods.\par
\par
\b 18. Configuration Update Test\b0\par
Edit a non-sensitive value in \f1 k8s/02-configmap.yaml\f0 , apply it, and restart the consumers:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/02-configmap.yaml\par
kubectl rollout restart deployment/user-service deployment/task-service deployment/stats-service -n taskflow\par
kubectl rollout status deployment/user-service -n taskflow --timeout=120s\par
\f0\par
This demonstrates that updated configuration is loaded when new containers start.\par
\par
\b 19. Troubleshooting\b0\par
Check logs:\par
\f1 kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/user-service\par
kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/task-service\par
kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/stats-service\par
\f0\par
Describe a failed Pod:\par
\f1 kubectl describe pod -n taskflow <pod-name>\par
kubectl get events -n taskflow --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\par
\f0\par
Common errors:\par
\tab \f1 CreateContainerConfigError\f0 : check Secret or ConfigMap names and keys.\par
\tab \f1 CrashLoopBackOff\f0 : check MongoDB credentials and application logs.\par
\tab Missing configuration: restart the Deployment after changing a ConfigMap or Secret.\par
\par
\b 20. Cleanup\b0\par
Delete the practical resources:\par
\f1 kubectl delete -f k8s/06-stats-service.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/05-task-service.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/04-user-service.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/03-mongodb.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/02-configmap.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/01-secret.yaml\par
\f0\par
Or delete the complete namespace:\par
\f1 kubectl delete namespace taskflow\par
\f0\par
\b 21. Result\b0\par
The containerized TaskFlow application successfully consumed non-sensitive values from a Kubernetes ConfigMap and sensitive MongoDB credentials from a Kubernetes Secret. The Deployments started successfully, health checks returned \f1 status: ok\f0 , and the configuration was verified inside running containers without exposing the password.\par
\par
\b 22. Conclusion\b0\par
Kubernetes ConfigMaps separate application configuration from container images, while Secrets protect sensitive values. Using \f1 configMapRef\f0 and \f1 secretKeyRef\f0 allows the same container images to run with environment-specific configuration without hardcoding settings in application code.\par
